<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tale of Aqhat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a> · Also known as Epic of Aqhat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tale of Aqhat is an epic poem from Ugarit, written in alphabetic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> on clay tablets of the 14th or 13th century BCE copied by the scribe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilimilku">Ilimilku</a>. It tells of the hero Aqhat, son of the righteous king Danel, who is killed after refusing to give his bow to the goddess Anat, and of his sister's vengeance. It is an important source for Canaanite religion and the background of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> poetry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tale-of-aqhat.jpg" alt="Tale of Aqhat" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rama · CC BY-SA 2.0 fr · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Danel_epic_AO17323_img_0160.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
